Update: I just realized I originally tested using a `string`, not a `ReadOnlySpan<char>`. I tested it again. I realized I cannot pass a char as an argument to `ROS.StartsWith`, only a ROS or a string.

I tested it with a string and it does have a perf regression:

```cs
private readonly string _myString = "MyString";

[Benchmark]
public bool FirstChar_StartsWith()
{
    ReadOnlySpan<char> s = _myString;
    return s.StartsWith(".");
}

[Benchmark]
public bool FirstChar_ManualCheck()
{
    ReadOnlySpan<char> s = _myString;
    return s.Length > 0 && s[0] == '.';
}
```

|                Method |      Mean |     Error |    StdDev |    Median |       Min |       Max | Gen 0 | Gen 1 | Gen 2 | Allocated |
|---------------------- |----------:|----------:|----------:|----------:|----------:|----------:|------:|------:|------:|----------:|
|  FirstChar_StartsWith | 3.0446 ns | 0.0192 ns | 0.0180 ns | 3.0483 ns | 3.0082 ns | 3.0689 ns |     - |     - |     - |         - |
| FirstChar_ManualCheck | 0.4466 ns | 0.0073 ns | 0.0069 ns | 0.4458 ns | 0.4338 ns | 0.4588 ns |     - |     - |     - |         - |

Even with a cached dot in a char array, the benchmark is still regressed considerably:

```cs
private readonly string _myString = "MyString";
private readonly char[] _c = { '.' };

[Benchmark]
public bool FirstChar_StartsWith()
{
    ReadOnlySpan<char> s = _myString;
    return s.StartsWith(_c);
}

[Benchmark]
public bool FirstChar_ManualCheck()
{
    ReadOnlySpan<char> s = _myString;
    return s.Length > 0 && s[0] == '.';
}
```

